## <h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Keeping Our Communities Clean</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> We Should Clean the Classroom on Friday.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Simplify complex instructions for classmates. communication: Confirm comprehension of peer contributions during discussions.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify complex instructions by using a combination of writing, visuals, symbols, and drawings to show steps (e.g., write 'recycle,' draw a recycling symbol, and point to a picture of a recycling bin). Can confirm understanding by repeating peer contributions and adding visuals (e.g., writing 'clean desks' and showing a cloth, saying, 'We clean here.'). Can clarify peer ideas, using visuals and simple phrases (e.g., writes 'need gloves' and points to gloves to confirm understanding).</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
    <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
    <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
    <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows two large pictures: one of a very messy classroom with trash on the floor and disorganized desks, and another of a sparkling clean and organized classroom. The teacher asks, "Look at these pictures. Which classroom is dirty? Which one is clean?" and "What should we do to make our classroom like the clean picture?" The teacher elicits answers like 'clean', 'pick up trash', 'organize desks'.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explains the concept of mediation by saying, "Sometimes instructions are long or difficult. Today, we will learn to make them simple and clear for our classmates." The teacher models simplifying: takes a complex instruction like "It is important for students to gather all the plastic bottles and paper for recycling." and simplifies it by writing 'Recycle bottles & paper' on the board, drawing a simple recycling symbol next to it, and pointing to recycling bins. The teacher then models confirming comprehension: "So, you mean we should collect the recycling?" (with a thumbs up). And clarifying: "Do we need gloves for picking up trash?" (showing a picture of gloves).</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do we do if instructions are difficult for our friends?" (Simplify, make them easy).</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can we make instructions easy to understand?" (Draw, use pictures, write simple words).</li>
      </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
    </td>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input:</b> The teacher displays a short, slightly complex written list of instructions for a classroom clean-up project, using some of the target grammar and vocabulary. Example: "Our classroom clean-up project will happen on Friday. First, all volunteers must bring trash bags. Second, we should collect all the trash from under desks and around the area. Next, you can organize the books on the shelves. Finally, we will sweep the floor carefully."</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short notice or a list of 4-5 written instructions on a poster or projector slide. The genre is 'instructions/plan', register is 'informal', format is a 'bulleted list'.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing plans and tasks: 'We will clean the classroom on Friday.' Making suggestions: 'We should clean the desks.' Asking for and giving information: 'Who will bring the cleaning supplies?'</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is this list of instructions about?" (Cleaning the classroom).</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> Students match a written instruction (e.g., "collect all trash") to a simple drawing or symbol provided by the teacher (e.g., a trash can icon).</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "We will clean on Saturday." (False). "Volunteers must bring bags." (True).</li>
      </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
    </td>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Mediation (simplifying, confirming, clarifying). Grammar: Modal verbs for suggestions, ability, and obligation (can, should, must), Future tense (will), Imperatives (bring, clean, pick up, organize). Vocabulary: community, trash, bags, recycling, supplies, tasks, group, clean, organize, work, help, dirty, responsible. Pronunciation: Word stress on imperative commands like 'bring' and 'clean'. Phonemic awareness: Identifying short /ɪ/ in 'bring' and long /iː/ in 'clean'.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
      <ol>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Simplifying Instructions):</b> The teacher provides students with a few more complex classroom clean-up instructions (e.g., "All team members are responsible for gathering all pieces of trash from the floor area."), one by one. Students work individually or in pairs to simplify each instruction using a maximum of 3-4 words, one visual/symbol, and one drawing (e.g., write 'Pick up trash,' draw a trash can). The teacher monitors and helps with vocabulary like 'responsibility', 'gather'. (Focus on words like 'bags', 'supplies', 'collect', 'sort').</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Confirming Comprehension):</b> In pairs, Student A reads a simplified instruction (e.g., "Clean desks!"). Student B practices confirming comprehension by repeating the key phrase and adding a visual or gesture (e.g., "Clean desks? We clean here!" while making a wiping motion on the desk or showing a cloth). The teacher explicitly encourages the use of body language and simple props.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Clarifying Ideas):</b> Using the simplified instructions from activity 1, students continue in pairs. Student A gives a simplified instruction (e.g., "Recycle plastic bottles"). Student B pretends not to fully understand and asks a clarifying question, using simple phrases and pointing to a visual/drawing (e.g., "What is recycle?" or "Where put plastic?" while pointing to a recycling bin drawing). Student A then clarifies using simple words or by pointing to their drawing/symbol. (e.g., Student B: "Do we need gloves?" and points to gloves. Student A: "Yes, we must wear gloves to be safe.").</li>
      </ol>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
    </td>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Group Presentation/Demonstration. Students work in small groups to mediate sections of their "Community Clean-Up Plan" for the classroom.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity:</b> Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students). Each group is assigned a specific task or area for the classroom clean-up (e.g., "Trash & Recycling," "Desks & Chairs Organization," "Floor & Supplies").</p>
      <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group discusses and creates 3-4 key instructions for their task/area, using imperatives and modal verbs (e.g., "We must bring recycling bags," "You should organize books," "Clean the area," "We will sweep the floor."). They write these instructions on a small "digital poster" (or a piece of paper acting as a digital poster mock-up).</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Next, they prepare to mediate these instructions by adding simple drawings, symbols, and keywords next to each step on their poster to make them easy to understand. For instance, for "Bring trash bags," they might draw a bag. For "Recycle paper," they might draw a recycling symbol.</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups then rotate to present their "clean-up plan section" to another group. One group presents their simplified instructions, and the "audience" group asks clarifying questions (e.g., "Who will bring supplies?" "What do we do with dirty surfaces?") and confirms understanding (e.g., "So, first we clean, then we organize?"). The presenting group uses their visuals and simple language to simplify, confirm, and clarify, ensuring the other group fully understands their part of the clean-up project. Students are encouraged to use phrases like 'We should...' 'You must...' 'We will...' and vocabulary like 'team', 'helpers', 'tasks'.</li>
      </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
    </td>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the group mediation activity.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students mediate their group's section of the "Community Clean-Up Plan" to another group, demonstrating simplification, confirmation, and clarification skills using visuals and simple language.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Mediation:</b> Simplifies complex instructions using a combination of writing, visuals, symbols, and drawings to show steps (e.g., writes 'recycle,' draws a recycling symbol, and points).</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Confirmation of Understanding:</b> Confirms understanding by repeating peer contributions and adding visuals (e.g., writing 'clean desks' and showing a cloth).</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarification of Ideas:</b> Clarifies peer ideas using visuals and simple phrases (e.g., writes 'need gloves' and points to gloves).</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Language:</b> Incorporates modal verbs (should, must, can) and imperatives (bring, clean, organize).</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Participates actively and works effectively with group members.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two short prompts before leaving.</p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one instruction you helped a classmate understand better today, and how did you do it?"</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which visual or drawing did you use to make a task clear for someone else?"</li>
      </ul>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-6 min</p>
    </td>
  </tr>

  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using pictures and simple words help you explain things to your classmates during the clean-up plan activity?"</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult about making instructions clear for others today?"</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> "Did students successfully simplify instructions for their peers using a combination of visuals, symbols, and simple language? Were they able to effectively confirm and clarify peer contributions during discussions, demonstrating the targeted mediation skills? What challenges did they face with the target vocabulary or grammar (modals, imperatives) during their mediation tasks? How can I provide more opportunities for authentic mediation in future lessons, perhaps with slightly more complex information?"</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "This lesson concludes our unit on 'Keeping Our Communities Clean.' Students have demonstrated strong collaboration and communication skills. Next, we will begin a new theme focusing on 'Healthy Eating Habits,' where we will continue to apply communication skills to discuss personal preferences and give simple advice."</p>
    </td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-4 min</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>